{:fh/id  "FH-CTRL-006"
 :fh/law "A buffered controller rendered with no :reset-key is refused loudly with :rf.error/view-control-reset-revision-missing naming the kind and the recovery — the reset generation is required, never optional and never inferred from the value — while the same control given a revision renders, and a non-buffered controller that never asks for one is untouched."

 :kind :my.ui/buffered-field

 ;; The refusal. Optional would be worse than absent: a control with no
 ;; revision buffers correctly right up to the first caller rejection and
 ;; then loses it, and no call site would show which kind it was.
 :missing-revision-error-id :rf.error/view-control-reset-revision-missing
 :recovery                  :supply-a-reset-key

 ;; The same catalogued ex-data slots the address refusal carries — one
 ;; shape for both of a controller's required props.
 :extra-keys [:kind :props :reason :recovery]

 ;; The message names the prop, why the value cannot serve as the signal,
 ;; and the literal escape for a caller that never resets. Substrings —
 ;; stable in MEANING, not in bytes.
 :message-mentions [":reset-key" "value-equality" "0"]

 ;; The control for the refusal: the SAME view, given both required
 ;; props, renders. Without it the rejection could be green because
 ;; nothing renders at all.
 :control    [:invoice 7 :amount]
 :value      "100.00"
 :reset-key  4

 ;; And a controller that is addressed but NOT buffered asks for no
 ;; revision, so the cost of the generation is paid only by controls that
 ;; actually hold a draft the caller can reject.
 :unbuffered-renders? true

 ;; A literal is a legitimate revision and says "do not externally reset
 ;; an active edit". It must render exactly like a moving one.
 :stable-literal 0}
